Absolutely, happy to help! Debounce is a really common utility. The idea is that you have a function that might be called many times in rapid succession (for example in response to a user typing) and you only want to actually execute the underlying behavior once things have settled down for a given amount of time. Here's a clean TypeScript implementation with proper typing using generics so it works with any function signature.

```ts
export function debounce<Args extends unknown[]>(fn: (...args: Args) => void, ms: number): (...args: Args) => void {
  let t: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return (...args: Args) => {
    if (t) clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}
```

